<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="font-size: 1.1em; color: #333;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Education and Learning</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Trimester, I Am Learning About...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Infer implicit information when listening to current events. Interactive: Contribute to simple discussions on a variety of issues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can infer simple implicit information from spoken narratives about educational-related activities by responding to guiding questions (e.g., "What topic is Ana learning about this trimester?" "She is learning about world history.").
Can follow and contribute to simple class discussions about education topics, showing comprehension of details (e.g., "What is Ana studying this trimester?" "She is learning computer science and English.").
Can listen for specific details in conversations about future studies (e.g., "What is Carlos planning to learn next trimester?" "History.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of various school `subjects` (e.g., math, history, computer science, English) and asks students: "Look at these pictures. What `subjects` are you `curious` about this `trimester`?" Students respond with simple answers. The teacher asks a few students: "What `skills` do you want to `develop` this `trimester`?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences aloud using the target grammar and vocabulary. "This `trimester`, I am `learning` about new teaching methods. I am interested in `learning` more about technology. I would `like` to `develop` my digital `skills`." The teacher clearly pronounces the new vocabulary: `trimester`, `subject`, `learn`, `study`, `skills`, `knowledge`, `classmate`, `schedule`, `educational`, `curious`. The teacher also models stress patterns in "I *am* `learning`" and "I *would like*".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `trimester` a day, a week, or a part of the school year?" (Expected: A part of the school year).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'what `subject` are you `learning`?', am I asking about your friend or about what you `study` in class?" (Expected: What you `study` in class).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input:</b> The teacher plays a short audio dialogue (approximately 60-90 seconds) between two `classmates`, Ana and Carlos, discussing their `schedule` and what they are `learning` this `trimester`. The dialogue explicitly includes the target vocabulary and grammar (Present continuous, gerunds, modals) in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Dialogue Snippet:
                <br>Ana: "Hey Carlos, what `subjects` are you `learning` this `trimester`?"
                <br>Carlos: "Hi Ana! Well, I am `studying` `history` and computer science. I'm also interested in `developing` my English `skills`."
                <br>Ana: "That's great! I'm `learning` advanced math and `educational` psychology. I would `like` to `achieve` good `knowledge` in both."
                <br>Carlos: "Wow, `educational` psychology sounds `curious`! Are you enjoying your `schedule`?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio dialogue (recording) between two `classmates` about their `educational` `schedule` for the `trimester`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Infer implicit information when listening to conversations about current events (e.g., what subjects students are `learning` and their `career goals`). Identify specific details about individuals' `learning` plans.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question (First Listening):</b> "What are Ana and Carlos mainly talking about?" (Expected: Their `subjects` or what they are `learning` this `trimester`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Second Listening):</b> Students listen again and decide if statements are True or False.
                    <br>a. Carlos is `learning` `history` this `trimester`. (True)
                    <br>b. Ana is not `curious` about `educational` psychology. (False)
                    <br>c. Carlos wants to `develop` his writing `skills`. (False - English `skills`)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: `Present continuous` for ongoing actions (`She is learning computer science`), `Gerunds` after prepositions (`interested in learning`), `Modals` for desires (`I would like to study math`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: `trimester`, `subject`, `learn`, `study`, `skills`, `knowledge`, `classmate`, `schedule`, `educational`, `curious`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Stress patterns in modals and continuous forms (`I *am* learning`, `I *would like*`); phonemic awareness: production and sound discrimination in minimal pair sounds (`/ɪ/` in "skill" versus `/iː/` in "skull", `/æ/` in "had" versus `/e/` in "head").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Listen and Complete:</b> The teacher provides a partial transcript of the audio dialogue. Students listen to the dialogue again and fill in the missing words (e.g., `learning`, `trimester`, `skills`, `would like`, `interested in`). The teacher pauses as needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Listen and Identify:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Focus:</b> The teacher reads out sentences. Students listen and raise one hand if it's an ongoing action (`Present Continuous`) or two hands if it's a desire (`Modal`). E.g., "My `classmate` *is `studying`* history." (1 hand) vs. "I *would `like` to `learn`* coding." (2 hands).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation Focus (Minimal Pairs):</b> The teacher says pairs of words with /ɪ/ vs /iː/ or /æ/ vs /e/ (e.g., "skill" / "skull", "had" / "head"). Students listen and circle the word they hear on a worksheet, then repeat after the teacher focusing on discrimination.</li>
                    </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction / Peer Check:</b> Students compare their completed transcripts and identified words with a `classmate`. They then take turns reading aloud the sentences they completed, paying attention to the stress patterns and pronunciation. They listen to each other and gently correct.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task:</b> "My `Classmate`'s `Trimester` `Schedule` and Goals"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Pair work and brief whole-class sharing. Students work in pairs. Each student receives a "Student Profile Card" with a different `educational` `schedule` and `learning goals` for the `trimester`. (e.g., Student A: "I am `learning` advanced biology and chemistry this `trimester`. I am interested in `developing` my research `skills` to `achieve` a `scholarship`." Student B: "I am `studying` world `history` and art. I would `like` to `learn` a new language `actively`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A reads their profile silently. Student B reads their profile silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A asks Student B questions about their profile, focusing on listening for details: "What `subjects` are you `learning` this `trimester`?" "What `skills` are you interested in `developing`?" "What `knowledge` would you `like` to `achieve`?" (Pragmatic: Asking about progress/goals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B listens and answers based on their card. Student A takes brief notes on what they hear.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students swap roles. Student B asks Student A, and Student A answers while Student B takes notes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After the pair interaction, each student briefly reports to the class (e.g., "My `classmate`, [Name], is `learning` [Subject 1] and [Subject 2] this `trimester`. They are interested in `developing` [Skill]."). This requires students to accurately recall and report information they heard.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Students are actively engaged in listening to and discussing personal `educational` plans and `learning goals` for the `trimester`, reflecting the theme "This Trimester, I Am Learning About..." within the "Education and Learning" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during pair work, focusing on students' ability to listen for specific details and infer simple implicit information (e.g., "Is the student engaged in listening?" "Can the student identify the `subjects` being discussed?" "Can the student report the `classmate`'s `learning goals`?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Match `Trimester` Plans." The teacher plays 3-4 short (20-30 seconds each) audio recordings of different students talking about their `educational` plans for the `trimester`. Each recording describes what they are `learning`, a `skill` they want to `develop`, and a `goal`. Students listen and match each audio to a corresponding picture or name on a worksheet (e.g., a student with a book, a student at a computer, a student painting). After matching, students answer one guiding question per audio about implicit information, e.g., "Based on what you heard, which subject is *most likely* related to `career goals` in science?" or "What `knowledge` does this student want to `achieve`?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate matching of audio to visual/name (listening for main idea and key details).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly answers guiding questions, showing inference of simple implicit information.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates comprehension of target vocabulary (`trimester`, `subject`, `learn`, `study`, `skills`, `knowledge`, `schedule`, `educational`) and grammar (`Present Continuous`, `Modals`, `Gerunds`) in context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows active listening (e.g., eye contact, nodding, asking clarifying questions if allowed).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two prompts before leaving:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases about `learning` did I `learn` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I understand the conversations about `trimester` `schedules`? (1 = not well, 5 = very well)."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to discussions about `learning` today, and what helped me understand better?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I `actively` practice my listening `skills` outside of class to better understand `educational` topics?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked `well` in this `listening` lesson? (e.g., "The audio quality was clear, and students were engaged with the `classmate` dialogue and activity.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled with inferring implicit information; I need to provide more explicit examples or sentence frames for answering inference questions in future lessons. Also, ensure more practice with the /ɪ/ vs /iː/ sounds.").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> "Today we practiced listening to `classmates` talk about their `educational` plans this `trimester`. In our next lesson, we will read a text about a student's `learning` journey, using many of the same `educational` words we `learned` today. We will focus on reading comprehension to understand their `schedule` and `career goals`."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[____ / ____]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>